{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-73245",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6 \\\\ 3(t)^{2}+2(1-t)^{2}+(t+2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2(1-2t+{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}+4t+4) & =6 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6 & =6 \\\\ 6{t}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solution $t=0$ back into the equations of the line.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=1-(0)=1 \\\\ z=(0)+2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the point is $(0,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,1,2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
